    "answer": {
      "short": "The half-life is $t=\\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}$. It depends only on $k$.",
      "worked": "At the half-life, $M=\\dfrac{M_0}{2}$. Since $M_0>0$, divide by $M_0$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{M_0}{2}&=M_0e^{-kt}\\\\\\frac12&=e^{-kt}\\\\\\ln\\!\\left(\\frac12\\right)&=-kt\\\\t&=\\frac{\\ln 2}{k}\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe half-life depends only on $k$, so it is independent of $M_0$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
